<section aria-labelledby="tailscale">
  <h2 id="tailscale">optional Tailscale setup (iOS)</h2>
  <p>
    If you choose Tailscale setup to find your computers, the iOS app
    starts an embedded Tailscale node and asks you to sign in to your
    Tailscale account. This is separate from an optional phux account.
    The node contacts Tailscale's control service to read your tailnet's
    device list. It runs while the device picker is open, not as the
    transport for your terminal sessions; those use your phone's own
    network connection or Tailscale tunnel.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tailscale receives the node's name (host name), public keys, IP
    addresses, and network and operating-system metadata, associated with
    your Tailscale account. This is a persistent node, not an ephemeral
    one. Its identity and authentication state are saved locally outside
    device backups so you do not have to sign in each time. The app
    disables Tailscale log uploads, but that does not prevent the identity
    and metadata exchange needed to join your tailnet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Signing out of Tailscale setup attempts to reset authentication on a
    running node, then removes its local state. Closing the picker only
    stops the node; it does not erase that state. Neither action guarantees
    deletion of the node record or other data held by Tailscale. Manage the
    node in your Tailscale admin console; Tailscale's handling and retention
    of its records are governed by its
    <a href="https://tailscale.com/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="device">
  <h2 id="device">what stays on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Saved servers, preferences, and app settings are stored locally. The
    apps include no analytics or crash-reporting SDK. Specific device
    features are used only when you invoke them:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Camera</strong>: to scan a pairing QR code. On Android,
      scanning uses the Google Play services code scanner, and the app
      receives only the decoded link.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Microphone and speech recognition</strong> (iOS): to dictate
      a message. Transcription runs on your device.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Face ID</strong> (iOS): to unlock the app when you turn on
      the optional app lock.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Local network</strong>: to reach your phux server.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Notifications that a session needs your attention can be generated
    locally on your device. In iOS builds configured for account-backed
    push, remote notifications use Apple's Push Notification service
    (APNs). Registration requires both a signed-in phux account and active
    Pro access. Where enabled, the app sends its APNs device token and a
    push grant to the account backend to route notifications to that
    device. The free iOS release has no account or subscription backend,
    so this push path is inactive; builds using the no-op registry send
    no registration to an account backend.
  </p>
  <p>
    On iOS, the app keeps Apple's MetricKit performance and hang reports
    in its own storage, capped at the 60 most recent files and excluded
    from backups. They contain timing and system diagnostics, not terminal
    content, and are not uploaded. The app's support report is sent only
    if you share it yourself. You can read it before you share it, and it
    excludes server names, tokens, account data, and terminal content.
  </p>
  <p>
    On Android, if you have turned on device backup, Android may include
    the app's local data in your Google account backup. That backup is
    between you and Google.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="updates">
  <h2 id="updates">iOS update checks</h2>
  <p>
    The iOS app checks for newer versions through the channel it was
    installed from. App Store builds query Apple's public lookup service
    at <code>itunes.apple.com</code>, sending the app's bundle ID and,
    when available, a country code. TestFlight builds request
    <code>https://phux.sh/api/ios/release</code>, which returns available
    beta versions. Development builds do not make these update checks.
  </p>
  <p>
    The receiving service sees your IP address and ordinary HTTP request
    metadata. Requests to <code>phux.sh</code> are subject to the
    <a href="#website">website processing</a> described below. These
    checks do not send terminal content, pairing tokens, or your phux
    account identity. Updates themselves are installed through the App
    Store or TestFlight.
  </p>
</section>
